WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.500 --> 00:00:07.276
Database access should keep data separate
from query structure.

2
00:00:07.356 --> 00:00:15.858
Parameterized queries and safe ORM
patterns help prevent SQL injection when used

3
00:00:15.938 --> 00:00:17.170
correctly.

4
00:00:17.250 --> 00:00:20.946
Raw SQL is not automatically wrong,

5
00:00:21.026 --> 00:00:28.788
but it deserves careful review because
string assembly, dynamic filters,

6
00:00:28.868 --> 00:00:35.890
and unchecked sort or column names can
change the meaning of a query.

7
00:00:35.970 --> 00:00:41.145
Least-privilege database accounts also
matter:

8
00:00:41.225 --> 00:00:49.850
the account used by the web app should not
have more capability than the application

9
00:00:49.930 --> 00:00:50.669
needs.

10
00:00:50.749 --> 00:00:59.004
Transactions, error handling, and
authorization are part of safe data access.

11
00:00:59.084 --> 00:01:07.092
A route should not update half of a
sensitive workflow and then fail silently.

12
00:01:07.172 --> 00:01:13.086
A database error should not leak internals
to the client.

13
00:01:13.166 --> 00:01:21.667
Most importantly, query results should
still respect the caller's permissions.

14
00:01:21.747 --> 00:01:30.125
Filtering by tenant, owner, or resource
permission should happen server-side,

15
00:01:30.205 --> 00:01:36.858
not in the browser after too much data has
already been returned.

16
00:01:36.938 --> 00:01:43.838
File uploads and file processing create
their own trust boundary.

17
00:01:43.918 --> 00:01:50.448
A safe Flask design considers size limits,
filename handling,

18
00:01:50.528 --> 00:01:57.427
storage location, content type
expectations, extension policy,

19
00:01:57.507 --> 00:02:05.269
scanning expectations, temporary file
cleanup, and downstream parsing.

20
00:02:05.349 --> 00:02:13.727
Filenames and metadata are user-controlled
and should not be trusted as proof of

21
00:02:13.807 --> 00:02:15.286
safe content.

22
00:02:15.366 --> 00:02:24.483
Storage paths should be constrained so
uploaded content cannot overwrite application

23
00:02:24.563 --> 00:02:28.629
files or escape intended directories.

24
00:02:28.709 --> 00:02:35.608
Serialization formats such as JSON, YAML,
XML, and CSV are useful,

25
00:02:35.688 --> 00:02:41.109
but parsers and loaders should be chosen
carefully.

26
00:02:41.189 --> 00:02:49.937
Unsafe deserialization at a high level is
the risk of converting untrusted data into

27
00:02:50.017 --> 00:02:58.272
behavior, objects, references, or resource
usage that the application did not

28
00:02:58.352 --> 00:02:59.214
intend.

29
00:02:59.294 --> 00:03:06.440
Defensive processing uses safe parser
modes, size and depth limits,

30
00:03:06.520 --> 00:03:13.789
clear schemas, and authorization around
import and export features.

31
00:03:13.869 --> 00:03:22.740
Data handling should preserve trust
boundaries from request to storage to response.
